9891 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049891 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049891 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049891 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049891 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049891 #edb/ref 1031 "Rotations as Linear Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049891 #edb/ref 1033 932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049891 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "1bf5a1e3-35e9-581e-b44a-fa6e4c7b4bff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050104 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050104 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050104 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050104 #edb/ref 1031 "Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vectors in 2D" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050104 #edb/ref 1033 1401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050104 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "2d9b53c7-d4e4-5c8d-ad01-c539428002fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050589 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050589 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050589 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050589 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050589 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050589 #edb/ref 1031 "Rotations of Geometric Figures" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050589 #edb/ref 1033 1517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050589 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "563983d1-03fb-584f-a90d-4641ce4f0309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092065 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092065 #edb/ref 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-5,-1), (1,4), (3,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,-5)$\n\n- $(4,-1)$\n\n- $(-5,-3)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a74be952-4dd4-5a7f-a8a4-de68977c5bb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092065 #edb/ref 1074 "The clockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $270^\\circ.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n![](images/7f/7f8b60362b08e038452b3454b1719c6f51df7d0129cc7d957e328a92b4f7e7c3.svg)\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-1,5), (4,-1), (-5,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092066 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092066 #edb/ref 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-1), (-3,5),$ and $(4,-3).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n\n- $(-5,3)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e945271e-2180-559b-9998-53ab9982043c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092066 #edb/ref 1074 "The counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n![](images/a5/a5b5bd722e0f84e175af70516d25edbcdbe55b48cbbb0ee7821d03272afac1ff.svg)\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,1), (3,-5), (-4,3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092114 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7186" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092114 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a4/a43a1658a16151c0306702959c50e8d0288077983ad50a084b39703e206469c0.png)\n\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ shown above. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$ given that \n![](images/ac/ac8c8fcdf75b72b65ceadb1d500625d4414264973876c10bf47c47a8e418ffd7.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092114 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7091b406-96d1-5663-9575-66f26c6a6267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092114 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/41/414cb72ff004fe4c5898a0e2c7b60b4b3651473e776388fad21efd8fc9ae6165.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $A\\mathbf{v},$ we need to rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b24d8d460b7833a49f2bd7183f0f4b5d22d94a7d3b1d3bb9eb51beff9eeb7ef6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1039 "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186092065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092223 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092224 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049420 #edb/ref 1759218