 #edb/ref 17592186349785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350945 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350945 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350945 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350945 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186349785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350945 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8d6b35cd-a0c3-54ea-97bc-22f429fc058b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351372 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351372 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351372 #edb/ref 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,1),(0,-3),(-1,3).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,-3)$\n- $(-4,-1)$\n- $(0,3)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351372 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "77695064-c91d-5069-813d-3934cbdfcb63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351372 #edb/ref 1074 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,-1),(0,3),(1,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351378 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351378 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351378 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351378 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/df/df7a1eb6aaf75cc611826af00ce066ed05440bb048ad79d8c0532007b9a89c3a.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351378 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1646920-50a4-5df7-bf43-1d46a76c244f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351378 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/77/77936c4115b37d79ae301a93b218ecd23114ae4ad42d8298b8f058c1f50e82a2.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians **clockwise** about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ **clockwise** by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e9/e941207a92a2b688428c55be3bc17856ea47fa2e11db8e3dc571b88ebbe8e3b1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420099 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420099 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420099 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10cf90fb-e29e-510d-8cf4-93075f7dd6be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218642010